<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seneca the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4 BCE – 65 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Lucius Annaeus Seneca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Annaeus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, Seneca the Younger, was a Stoic philosopher, statesman and dramatist of Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, born at Corduba in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and trained in rhetoric and philosophy. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> in 41 CE, he was recalled in 49 to tutor the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, and after Nero became emperor in 54 he served as his adviser, with the praetorian prefect Burrus. His influence waned, and in 65 he was forced to take his own life for alleged complicity in the Pisonian conspiracy, of which he may have been innocent. His essays and 124 letters are among the most important sources for ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, and his tragedies include Medea, Thyestes and Phaedra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger.jpg" alt="Seneca the Younger" width="330" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Calidius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duble_herma_of_Socrates_and_Seneca_Antikensammlung_Berlin_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
